'''Errors''' in the game are messages which are generated when something unexpected happens and are logged inside the [[console]]. Errors can come from different sources, either from your [[mods]] or even from the base game itself in some cases. They will appear on the bottom right with a red popup telling you how many errors have been logged, but in some cases some of these can be silent errors which will not show up in the popup.

Errors can come from different aspects of the game:
* [[Lua (API)|Lua]]
* [[Java]]
* [[AnimSets]]
* [[Modeling|Model]] assets
* [[Texturing|Texture]] assets
* [[Animation]] assets

This wiki page will try to document every error that can be generated by the game, but it will be incomplete.

== Reading errors ==
[[File:PZ Modding Configs - preview.png|thumb]]
[[File:ErrorMagnifier - preview1.jpeg|thumb]]

You have multiple solutions available when it comes to reading those errors:
* [[Console]] files
* [[errorMagnifier]]
* [[Console#In-game console|In-game console]] {{Sup|not recommended|red}}
* [[Lua debugger#Errors|Lua debugger]] {{Sup|not recommended|red}}

[[errorMagnifier]] is perfect for the users, because it provides nice tools to read the errors and format them in a bug report to send to the modders. Getting used to reading the [[console]] files is a key skill for modders, because it will help them understand what is going on in the game. This is made even easier with the use of [[PZ Modding Configs]] whichs provides syntax highlight solutions for the [[console]] files.
{{Clear}}
== Lua ==
Lua errors are often of different types:
* syntax mistakes (e.g. missing a closing bracket, missing a comma, missing <code>end</code> statement, etc.)
* unexistent API calls (e.g. calling a function that does not exist, or calling a function with the wrong parameters)

Setting up [[EmmyLua]] with [[Umbrella (modding)|Umbrella]] can help you catch these errors before they happen, most particularly with syntax mistakes. Using [[typing]]s will help catch early the second type of errors almost all of the time.

=== Error format ===
{{Stub|This part needs to describe the format errors take in the [[console]] files, and how to read them.}}

=== Error list ===
Below is a list of some of the Lua errors that you can encounter:
{| class="wikitable theme-blue sortable" style="text-align: left;
|+ style="white-space:nowrap" | Modding fields
|-
! Error message !! Description
|-
|
{{CodeSnip
| code =
Tried to call nil
}}
|
This error triggers when you try to call a function that does not exist. "calling" refers to the act of executing a function, so when that function doesn't exist it is simply a <code>nil</code> variable.

For example:
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
local function myFunction()
    print("Hello world!")
end

-- myFunction is a variable containing a function
-- so it can be called
myFunction() -- works

-- if we try the same on a non-existing function, it will trigger the error
unexistingFunction() -- errors
}}

|-
|
{{CodeSnip
| code =
Attempted index of non-table
}}
|
This error happens when you try to index ({{Code|lang=lua|tbl[1]}} or {{Code|lang=lua|tbl.field}}) a variable which is not a table.

For example:
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
local myTable = {1, 2, 3}
print(myTable[1]) -- works

local myNumber = 5
print(myNumber[1]) -- errors
}}

|-
|
{{CodeSnip
| code =
Unexpected symbol near 'something'
}}
|
This triggers when you made a syntax mistake in your Lua code, where the <code>something</code> part is replaced by the symbol that is unexpected. For example, an extra bracket, comma, or letter where it shouldn't be will trigger this error.

For example:
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
function abc()) end -- unexpected symbol near ')'

local a = 5
a l= 15 -- unexpected symbol near 'l'

local a = 5] -- unexpected symbol near ']'
}}

|-
|
{{CodeSnip
| code =
Attempt to index a nil value (global 'something')
}}
|
Triggered when you try to index a variable which is <code>nil</code>, be it an indefined global or local.

For example:
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
abc.x = 5 -- abc is nil, error

abc = {} -- abc defined here

abc.x = 5 -- works

xyz['x'] = 'abc' -- xyz is nil, error
}}

|-
| 
{{CodeSnip
| code =
__len not defined for operand
}}
|
This error happens when you try to retrieve the length of a variable ({{Code|lang=lua|#variable}}) which cannot be measured. For example, trying to get the length of an [[IsoZombie]] instance, or even of a float will trigger this error.

For example:
{{CodeSnip
| lang = lua
| code =
local myTable = {1, 2, 3}
local length = #myTable -- works

local myNumber = 5.0
local length = #myNumber -- errors
}}

|-
|
{{CodeSnip
| code =
Can't set metatable for nil
}}
|
This is only a fairly advanced error which involves the use of [https://www.lua.org/manual/5.1/manual.html#2.8 Metatables]. It happens when you try to set a metatable for a variable which is <code>nil</code>.
|}

== Scripts ==
{{Stub|Errors due to scripts syntax mistakes should be documented here.}}
Errors from [[scripts]] are triggered by the parser itself. [[ZedScripts]] can help catch these errors early before they happen.

